  "summary": "Hand-built Berber jars painted in black-on-red geometry and lime-white symbol fields.",
  "description": "Kabyle women build water jars, oil lamps and couscoussiers by hand without a wheel, then paint them in the Kabyle palette: terracotta slip, lime white and black manganese, in geometric fields of chevrons, crossed lines, dots and Berber fertility symbols believed to bless the household. Made for dowries and village fountains, the pottery survived colonial neglect and mid-century decline through dedicated regional cooperatives. Kabyle jars now stand as icons of Amazigh material culture.",
